 @doctorthee  Most coding now-a-days is just a script or a series of scripts. No one is saying that this is super military grade secret spy software. Also, if it only takes a dev team a few dedicated hours to automate a process to break CAPTCHA, then it's not that hard to defeat. Many can get around this by having Chinese slave labor to physically work through the captcha manually for pennies. The hard part, getting the foot in the door with the product in cart was already done and you've at that point have probably killed the network from receiving any new connections for a while so you have a little time at that point. 

There are some very complex systems out there though that use neural networks and machine learning to actually identify the pictures with the given keywords and they've been trained with nearly every mundane, common object you can find. Ironically, it's because of the efforts of Nvidia to hand out tensor cores that's made it easy and affordable yet powerful enough to do commonly now. 

The best way to defeat an automated system using a complex neural net is to use contextual systems instead of directly obvious ones since machine learning still can't really deal with context yet. This gets difficult because some people can't deal with it easily either sometimes and you risk losing customers.

 @doctorthee  There have been articles over a year old now that demonstrated a general desktop PC beating CAPTCHA. It's the reason why the company that founded CAPTCHA announced that they were upgrading to a newer standard and methodology. 
https://medium.com/@ageitgey/how-to-break-a-captcha-system-in-15-minutes-with-machine-learning-dbebb035a710
Holy hell it took me less than a second and this article is from 2017. It's not irrelevant to this current situation. Several of these companies specializing in running these bots have already made the claim, whether it's true or not is not the issue, that they have already integrated DL AI to get around CAPTCHA. There ARE retailers that use CAPTCHA to stop the bots and they were still botted out. Now there are some of those networks that have a staff of cheap practically slave labor to manually do the CAPTCHA for them. Either way though, the bots can generally effectively lock most of the competition out just by over saturating the retailer's network where the network is overwhelmed and can't issue new connections for a period of time.
